column3:
Independent podcasters and small podcast teams whose episodes occasionally or regularly contain explicit language and who handle at least part of post-production themselves.

They care about producing a polished version of an episode, but do not want to spend time manually locating every explicit word and placing bleeps or edits one by one.

Particularly relevant to creators who:
– monetize or intend to monetize their content through advertising or sponsorships;
– want the option to produce a cleaner version of an episode for wider distribution;
– regularly publish conversational, comedy, interview, commentary, or other unscripted formats where explicit language naturally occurs;
– do not have a dedicated audio engineer handling every episode.

The user can currently:
– manually find and censor explicit language in an audio editor;
– use professional editing software that supports automated or bulk censoring (i.e. Adobe Premiere);
– use a dedicated profanity-censoring service;
– outsource creation of a clean version;
– or skip producing a clean version altogether.

Cast makes censoring part of the same workflow used to edit and finish the podcast.

It can:
– automatically detect explicit language;
– flag occurrences directly in the transcript;
– censor detected words without manually locating them on a waveform;
– support custom words and phrases;
– replace censored speech with different sounds rather than only a standard beep.

The campaign should reach people who are already involved in podcast creation rather than a broad audience interested in podcasts as listeners.

Potential targeting signals include:
– podcast creation / podcast editing;
– audio editing and creator tools;
– podcast hosting and recording platforms;
– creator / content-production interests;
– contextual placements around podcast-production education;
– search or intent signals around censoring, bleeping, explicit-language editing, and podcast editing.

Exact targeting should be defined per media channel.
